  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions & Synoptic Overview:\nA broad ridging pattern sits over the central CONUS aloft, with\na mid-level shortwave impinging into the zonal flow along the\nnorthern periphery of the ridge across Iowa and northern\nIllinois. Remnant showers associated with this shortwave have\nslowly pushed into northwestern Illinois as the day has\nprogressed, but organization and intensity continues to wane as\nshowers become removed from their forcing mechanism.\nAdditionally, the 12Z ILX sounding indicates the presence of an\nEML, and any precip that falls will need to fall through\n15,000ft of dry air without evaporating to reach the surface,\nwhich appears rather unlikely (5-10% chance along and north of\nI-74, <5% elsewhere). However, the system has brought widespread\nhigh cloud cover to the area, which will linger into tonight.\nThe evaporative cooling from the virga should also permit 15-20mph\nwind gusts to mix down to the surface this afternoon.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures will reach the low to mid-80s this\nafternoon, with the warmest temperatures in areas furthest to\nthe south and west. Lows tonight fall to the upper 60s east of\nI-55 and into the low 70s along and west of I-55.\n\nWarm Front Raises Temps Sunday:\nAs the 850mb high pressure sags south and east, strong\nsouthwesterly flow from the Plains advects warmer temperatures\ninto the region late tonight into Sunday morning. The warm front\npushes to the north of the area by late morning, increasing\ntemperatures into the low to mid-90s Sunday afternoon and\nbringing heat indices into the mid- to upper 90s. \n\nLow Rain Chances Sunday Morning:\nAs the warm front pushes through the area early Sunday morning,\nthe nose of the nocturnal LLJ may overlap with energy from\nanother, slightly stronger shortwave that rides along the top of\nthe ridge again tonight, which would allow some scattered\nconvection to develop. However, the moisture that will ride\nalong the southerly wind will lag behind the initial front a\nbit, so there will still be a significant layer of dry air in\nthe low-levels that precip will need to overcome before rain\nreaches the ground. For that reason, PoPs between 7-10AM max out\nbetween 10-25%, with the best chances being furthest north of\nthe I-74 corridor. \n\nHot All Week:\nMain weather story continues to be the incoming heat that will\nlast through the duration of the week. Ensembles remain in good\nagreement that temperatures will increase into the mid- to upper\n90s on Sunday and remain there through at least Wednesday as the\nridge stays parked over the southeast US. Temperatures approach\ntriple digits Tuesday and Wednesday, with the probability for\nheat indices >100 degrees starts creeping up in the far southern\ncounties on Tuesday (35-60%) per the LREF, further increasing to\nwidespread 30-75% on Wednesday and remaining at 30-60% on\nThursday. In addition to warm daytime highs, the low temperature\novernight only drops into the mid- to upper 70s on Tuesday and\nWednesday. The long duration of anomalously warm temperatures\nfor early September and lack of overnight relief starts pushing\nthe area into Major (Level 3 of 4) to Extreme (Level 4 of 4)\nHeat Risk by mid-week, and remains elevated as the holiday\nweekend approaches.\n\nShould Stay Dry Most of the Week:\nCentral Illinois should remain near enough to the center of the\nridge in zonal flow to avoid most of the nocturnal MCS activity\nthat will result from repeated shortwaves riding along the\nnorthern edge of the ridge, but low (<5%) PoPs in the far\nnorthern reaches of the CWA could increase if the\nstrength/amplitude of the ridge is being overdone in the global\nmodels or if the undercutting shortwaves come in a little\nstronger than what is currently being modeled. By Wednesday and\nThursday, guidance indicates that the ridging over the southeast\nUS starts breaking down, opening the door to increased rain\nchances Thursday and beyond.",
